# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_FUTEX_WAIT */
int64_t futex_wait(uint32_t *value, uint32_t current, uint32_t new_owner,
                   uint64_t deadline);
/* CROI_SYS_FUTEX_WAKE */
int64_t futex_wake(uint32_t *value, uint64_t count);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A futex is a 32-bit word in user memory that threads of one process wait on.
The kernel keeps nothing for it until a thread waits; locks are built on it so that they cost no call while uncontended.
The futex is named by the address of the word in the caller's address space.
<value> must be 4-byte aligned and inside user memory (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).

`futex_wait` blocks the calling thread if the word at <value> is <current>, and returns `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` at once if it is not.
It returns 0 when woken by `futex_wake`, `CROI_ERR_TIMED_OUT` at <deadline> and `CROI_ERR_CANCELED` if the thread is killed.
If the word is paged out it is paged in first.
A suspension (task_suspend(2)) of the waiting thread ends the wait and the call is made again when the thread resumes.

<new_owner> is a handle to a thread, or 0 for none.
A thread named as the owner runs with the scheduling parameters of the threads that wait, while it holds the futex, so a lock's holder is not kept off the CPU by lower-priority threads.
The handle needs no particular right.
The caller itself as owner is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
A thread that is not running has nothing to lend to, and is no owner.
The futex has no owner after a `futex_wake`, and once its last waiter has gone.
The only call that takes a handle is the one with an owner; with <new_owner> 0 the call works in any process.

`futex_wake` wakes up to <count> threads waiting on <value>, the longest waiting first, and clears the futex's owner.
It returns 0, whether or not any thread was waiting; it does not report how many woke.

